@page "/developer/api"
@using Microsoft.Extensions.Options
@inject IOptions<PortalOptions> Options
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "API and feeds";
    var site = Options.Value.PublicUrl.Length > 0 ? Options.Value.PublicUrl.TrimEnd('/') : $"{Request.Scheme}://{Request.Host}";
}
<p class="crumbs"><a href="/developer">Developer</a> / API and feeds</p>
<h1>API and feeds</h1>
<p class="lead">For tools, agents and feed readers. Read-only, no key, JSON or Atom. Channels themselves are plain files: pkg reads them directly.</p>

<h2 class="label">Reading a channel</h2>
<pre><code>curl '@site/pkg/index'
curl '@site/pkg/withdrawals'</code></pre>
<p>A channel is the directory channel byte for byte: <code>index</code>, one line per published version and CPU, and <code>objects/&lt;digest&gt;.manifest</code>, <code>.sig</code>, <code>.pkg</code> beside it. <code>withdrawals</code> names the versions whose publisher withdrew them, header first and one digest per line, so a reader asks for a signed <code>.withdrawn</code> file only where there is one instead of once per entry. It is written by the portal and empty when nothing is withdrawn; what it names is still proven by the signed file. <a href="/docs/channels">Channels</a> describes the format in full.</p>
<p>Take a channel one file at a time, as pkg does. An address may have eight requests in flight at once; more than that wait their turn and are served. A refusal from the parts of the site that do count requests, such as this API, answers <code>429</code> with <code>Retry-After</code> and a record saying to wait a minute.</p>

<h2 class="label">Search</h2>
<pre><code>curl '@site/api/search?q=rexx'</code></pre>
<p>The results of the search page, as JSON. Parameters, all optional: <code>q</code>, <code>channel</code>, <code>kind</code>, <code>arch</code>, <code>tag</code>, <code>category</code>, <code>sort</code> (<code>newest</code> or <code>downloads</code>; relevance otherwise), <code>take</code> (1 to 500, 100 by default). Each result gives the channel, name, latest version, kind, CPUs, short description, category, tags, the libraries it provides, authors, licence, signer, last update, downloads, why it matched when not by name, its page, its channel address and the command that installs it.</p>

<h2 class="label">One package</h2>
<pre><code>curl '@site/api/packages/pkg/pkg'</code></pre>
<p>The same fields, plus every version with its CPU, the date the portal first published it, the manifest and payload digests, the signer, downloads, and whether its publisher withdrew it.</p>

<h2 class="label">This portal's rules</h2>
<pre><code>curl '@site/api/policy'</code></pre>
<p>What this instance accepts: uploads at all, binaries or links only, which archive hosts, whether a push may create a channel, plain http. A refusal caused by one of these rules names it (<code>policy: Binaries=off</code>) with the operators' note.</p>

<h2 class="label">Feeds</h2>
<p>Atom, the 50 newest versions first: <a href="/feed">every channel</a>, or one channel at <code>/channels/&lt;name&gt;/feed</code>, such as <a href="/channels/contrib-nightly/feed">contrib-nightly</a>.</p>

<h2 class="label" id="badges">Badges</h2>
<p>Share a package from a README, website or forum with a linked image. Choose a small version badge or a large download badge. Copy an image URL for the badge alone, or use the Markdown or HTML widget to make it clickable. Widgets open the package’s page at its installation command.</p>
@foreach (var size in new[] { "small", "large" })
{
    var imageUrl = $"{site}/badge/pkg.svg" + (size == "large" ? "?size=large" : "");
    var link = $"{site}/packages/pkg/pkg#install";
    var markdown = $"[![Get pkg]({imageUrl})]({link})";
    var html = $"<a href=\"{link}\"><img src=\"{imageUrl}\" alt=\"Get pkg\" /></a>";
    <h3>@(size == "large" ? "Large" : "Small") badge and widget</h3>
    <p><a href="@link"><img src="@imageUrl" alt="Get pkg" style="max-width:100%;height:auto" /></a></p>
    <div class="cmd wrapcode"><code>@imageUrl</code><button class="copy" data-copy="@imageUrl">Copy image URL</button></div>
    <div class="cmd wrapcode"><code>@markdown</code><button class="copy" data-copy="@markdown">Copy Markdown</button></div>
    <div class="cmd wrapcode"><code>@html</code><button class="copy" data-copy="@html">Copy HTML</button></div>
}
<p>Replace <code>pkg</code> with your package name. Each <a href="/search">package page</a> supplies ready-to-copy badges with its channel included, so the link identifies the intended package.</p>
<p class="muted small"><code>/badge/&lt;channel&gt;/&lt;name&gt;.svg</code> serves the image and <code>/b/&lt;channel&gt;/&lt;name&gt;</code> is its link. Add <code>?size=large</code> to the image for the large format. Both routes also accept a name alone. The existing <code>/b/&lt;name&gt;</code> image address supports both sizes and redirects browser visits to the package's installation command.</p>

<h2 class="label">How pkg spreads</h2>
<p><a href="/api/usage">/api/usage</a> hands over every tally the portal keeps: for each day, how many channel reads, packages taken and pushes came from each pkg version, system and CPU. That is all of it; there is nothing per request, per address or per person. <a href="/privacy">Privacy</a> says what is kept and what is not.</p>

<h2 class="label">Downloads counted</h2>
<p>A signed package the portal serves, fetched from its first byte, counts once; so does each way of getting pkg. Archives kept by their makers, such as the nightly contrib build, are not seen by the portal and not counted.</p>
